title Sixty-four names a family
prompt Does 64 work for SHA3-256, and is there a case for SHA3-512?
think YES, AND THE QUESTION CORRECTS SOMETHING I WROTE. I had said exact-64 commits to SHA-256. It does not: 64 hex characters is the width of ANY 256-bit digest -- SHA-256, SHA3-256 and BLAKE2b-256 alike, each 32 bytes. So the length names a FAMILY rather than an algorithm, and the option is wider than I made it sound.
think SHA3-512 IS 128 HEX RATHER THAN 64, so it is a different row rather than the same row with another name. The arithmetic: 512 bits, 64 bytes, 128 hex characters.
obs THE CASE FOR SHA3-512 IS REAL AND TREE-NATIVE, which is why it deserved the ask. crypto/sha3.rye, crypto/sha3_digest.rye and crypto/keccak256.rye already stand written; SHA3-512 already seals construction/waymark-registry.bron and addresses content in Aurora. And the DISC ladder signs with SLH-DSA-SHAKE-256s, where SHAKE is SHA3's own extendable-output function -- so choosing SHA3 anywhere in this receipt puts the whole object in ONE cryptographic family rather than two.
obs THE CASE AGAINST IS THE FACT'S OWN CEILING. 128 hex costs twice the bytes inside a 4,096-byte encoded fact that a post-quantum signature is already crowding, and 256-bit collision resistance is ample for the content digest of a small data product.
obs SO THE PROPOSAL SHARPENS RATHER THAN MOVES: exact 64, with the scheme NAMED as SHA3-256 in the row, and the row written as a 256-bit digest in hex so the width outlives the algorithm. It keeps the Keccak family the tree's own seal and signature ladder already live in, matches the fixture at 64 hex, and costs half of SHA3-512.
obs THE CORRECTION IS THE POINT OF THE LAP. A reader of the earlier row would have believed choosing 64 foreclosed SHA3, and it never did. Being asked a sharp question is how that surfaced.
loom sha256_hex=64 sha3_256_hex=64 blake2b256_hex=64 sha3_512_hex=128 fixture_hex=64
